  "method": [
    "Compare four independent model processes with four threads holding private engine/model state and sharing one set of immutable ONNX sessions. One ORT thread per inference.",
    "Nano uses twelve public sentences alternating two bundled voices, four inference steps and per-input seeds100+i. Turbo uses four public sentences alternating two bundled voices and benchmark-only greedy temperature0 for byte-exact reference comparison; production retains SDKdefaulttemperature0.8.",
    "Each benchmark has one cold wave and two warm waves, with new inference every time and no audio/checkpoint reuse. Peak RSS sums parent and descendants; operating-system caches are not reset.",
    "All concurrent Nano/Turbo waveforms are byte-identical to independent serial results under these deterministic comparison settings. Native Windows synthesis and shared concurrency are checked separately by the release workflow."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Short-sample results on the cloud host do not establish performance for a full hour, all speakers/languages, natural-sampling Turbo or the user computer.",
    "Both engines show substantially lower measured RAM. Their warm synthesis throughput was essentially unchanged within about2percent; do not claim a warm speed increase from sharing alone.",
    "Nano cold timing varied and was slower in this pair. Turbo showed a faster cold sample in one clean pair; neither establishes a universal startup improvement.",
    "The first Turbo shared cold sample overlapped a brief browser test, and the first Turbo process sample overlapped unit tests. Those timing runs are excluded; clean repeats are reported.",
    "Serial Turbo timing partly overlapped unit tests and is used only as the deterministic audio parity reference, never as a performance baseline.",
    "No private voice source recordings or generated waveforms are redistributed. Public preset names and synthetic input hashes are verification metadata."
  ],
